(registered 2026-10-13, last updated 2026-10-13)

Scheme name: opns

Status: provisional

Applications/protocols that use this scheme name:
Wallet applications on the ORDnet stack that support OpNS names
(dot-less on-chain names, e.g. opns:alex). "opns" expresses a payment
intent to the current holder of an OpNS name, resolved via an OpNS
index and always confirmed explicitly by the user before any signing
operation. Specified in ODNCA-STD-011 (public draft); this registration
will be updated as that specification is adopted.

Contact: Alexander Taytelbaum, ODNCA — ORDnet Decentralized Names
Coordination Authority, info&odnca.org

Change controller: ODNCA (odnca.org)

References:
- ODNCA-STD-011 "WEB3-ACTIONS-1 - The URI Scheme Family" (public draft
v0.5) — https://odnca.org/docs/ODNCA-STD-011-WEB3-ACTIONS-1.pdf

Scheme syntax:
opns:<name>
<name> is an OpNS name: UTF-8, contains no dot, no whitespace or
control characters. Colon-only form; producers MUST NOT emit "opns://".
No DNS interaction at any point.

Scheme semantics: a payment intent addressed to the name's current
holder; the handling wallet resolves the name, displays the resolved
address, and requires explicit user confirmation. Never executed
automatically.

Encoding considerations: UTF-8 names; percent-encoding per RFC 3986.
No IDNA/punycode processing.

Interoperability considerations: no DNS resolution; unaware
applications treat it as an unknown scheme per RFC 3986. No authority
component, no default port.

Security considerations: OpNS name state is not yet covered by ODNCA's
committed-state anchoring (the specification tracks implementation
status honestly in its §9); clients therefore resolve via an index and
MUST display the resolved address before any signing operation, and
MUST NOT auto-sign. Visually confusable Unicode names carry the usual
homograph risks.
